177
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:58,740
And I want to make sure to expand upon the list and explain exactly why we chose some of the stuff we chose.

178
00:10:58,860 --> 00:11:02,840
But I will get that done within the next several days, Mark, and I appreciate the question.

179
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:07,860
Well, like I promised, we have an interview coming up.

180
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,540
It's a little bit longer than normal, but I think you'll find it's worth your time.

181
00:11:12,260 --> 00:11:14,300
And I think we need to get started.

182
00:11:14,300 --> 00:11:15,000
Shall we?

183
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:40,340
Hey, this is Clark Green with the Scoutmaster Podcast, and I am happy to have on Skype with me Rob Ferris and Harry Wimbrough,

184
00:11:40,340 --> 00:11:45,480
two of the three authors of a book called Working the Patrol Method.

185
00:11:45,740 --> 00:11:46,960
How are you guys doing this evening?

186
00:11:47,220 --> 00:11:47,840
We're great, Clark.

187
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:48,340
We're great, Clark.

188
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:53,620
So, Rob, you've got five kits, from what I can tell you.

189
00:11:53,620 --> 00:11:55,280
That's right, I do. I do.

190
00:11:55,800 --> 00:12:00,200
One of them is an Eagle Scout, and the others are on the way, huh?

191
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:01,620
Well, except for my daughter.

192
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:03,640
Well, yeah, yeah.

193
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:07,880
She was in part inspiration for what we wrote.

194
00:12:07,880 --> 00:12:13,660
But she's been a constant supporter, and she actually reviewed a part of the books.

195
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:17,720
You became an Eagle Scout back in the late 60s, early 70s.

196
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:21,900
And then when your oldest son turned 11, you got back in.

197
00:12:22,240 --> 00:12:25,040
Just briefly tell me what this statement in the book means.

198
00:12:25,140 --> 00:12:27,060
It says, Wood Badge changed your life.

199
00:12:27,060 --> 00:12:33,740
Wood Badge was what made me finally get the patrol method inside and out.

200
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:36,200
And it was just an incredible experience for me.

201
00:12:36,520 --> 00:12:41,220
It allowed us to actually practice the patrol method as adults,

202
00:12:41,440 --> 00:12:45,740
and then to step back and actually realize,

203
00:12:45,900 --> 00:12:48,300
okay, how do we create this kind of teamwork?

204
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:54,180
How do we create this kind of camaraderie with our scouts back in our units?

205
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:56,300
And it was an amazing experience.

206
00:12:56,300 --> 00:13:00,800
Let me also introduce Harry, Harry Wimbrough.

207
00:13:01,420 --> 00:13:05,420
And you also became an Eagle Scout in the mid-70s?

208
00:13:05,640 --> 00:13:06,819
Yes, 1975.

209
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:12,099
When you wrote the introduction to this book, your son Joe was on his way.

210
00:13:12,719 --> 00:13:13,780
Has he made it yet?

211
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:15,060
Not yet.

212
00:13:15,500 --> 00:13:20,120
I keep reminding him on a daily basis that his time is running out.

213
00:13:20,120 --> 00:13:29,680
He's a senior in high school, plays in three different bands, plays the flute, and has a girlfriend.

214
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:34,200
So I'm reminded that his time is running out.

215
00:13:35,660 --> 00:13:40,580
You and two million other dads are doing the exact same thing.

216
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:49,640
Well, even though I made my Eagle in 75, what a lot of people don't know is I set my board,

217
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:56,980
or got presented my Eagle after I had already completed basic and AIT for the Army.

218
00:13:57,140 --> 00:13:57,680
Oh, no kidding.

219
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:00,200
So you were right up against it too, huh?

220
00:14:00,660 --> 00:14:04,680
Oh, yes, I was right up against it.

221
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:07,440
Well, you know, your secret is safe with me.

222
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:08,860
I will not tell Joe that.

223
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:12,540
I'll bet he already knows, though.

224
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:13,640
He already knows.

225
00:14:15,660 --> 00:14:20,620
Oh, I want to also mention Ted Knight, who is one of your co-authors.

226
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:24,980
You had the three of you author this, and unfortunately Ted couldn't join us this evening.

227
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:31,640
The book is called Working the Patrol Method, and there was a specific reason that you stayed in the book

228
00:14:31,640 --> 00:14:37,140
for having gotten it started and taken it all the way through to press.

229
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:40,160
Tell me, guys, who is Bryson Bort?

230
00:14:40,160 --> 00:14:43,760
Bryson Bort is an Eagle alumni of our unit.

231
00:14:44,140 --> 00:14:50,939
He joined the United States Army, was stationed with the Army where he was exercising a leadership

232
00:14:50,939 --> 00:14:51,520
position.

233
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:58,980
As he got into that and started trying to do his job effectively, what he realized was that

234
00:14:58,980 --> 00:15:06,480
many, many things that he learned in Boy Scouts were invaluable to him in assisting him and

235
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:09,580
informing him as to how to be a leader in the United States Army.

236
00:15:09,580 --> 00:15:15,980
You begin in the first pages of the book with an email that you got from Bryson.

237
00:15:16,440 --> 00:15:19,620
It is a very inspirational introduction to the book.

238
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:21,320
It really kind of sets the tone.

239
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:27,580
Why don't you describe exactly what you were after in going through the work to write this book?

240
00:15:27,640 --> 00:15:28,620
It must have taken ages.

241
00:15:28,620 --> 00:15:33,180
It was a labor of love, Clark, but it actually literally took some years.

242
00:15:34,080 --> 00:15:41,220
It took us about four years to put it all together.

243
00:15:41,220 --> 00:15:48,760
The stories that are in the book are actual stories that we could take and mold and write

244
00:15:48,760 --> 00:15:55,320
about how the adult leader coached and mentored and taught the young scout.

245
00:15:55,420 --> 00:15:56,780
What do we call those in the book here?

246
00:15:56,860 --> 00:15:57,820
Campfire yarns?

247
00:15:58,200 --> 00:15:59,100
Yeah, yarns.

248
00:15:59,200 --> 00:16:02,620
Just as Maiden Powell called his stories yarns.

249
00:16:02,680 --> 00:16:04,640
We adopt his terminology.

250
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:10,660
They are tremendously helpful in kind of putting some meat on the bones of the concepts that

251
00:16:10,660 --> 00:16:15,540
you're talking about because a lot of what was in them was very familiar to me as a scout

252
00:16:15,540 --> 00:16:15,900
master.

253
00:16:16,240 --> 00:16:20,040
Pretty typical stories that happen in scout troops all over the country.

254
00:16:20,400 --> 00:16:27,400
Actually, a lot of what scout masters will see, or I'm experienced scout masters will see

255
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:29,660
in our book, as you say, Clark, will be very familiar.

256
00:16:29,660 --> 00:16:35,579
It'll be situations that they've confronted themselves or that they've seen other leaders

257
00:16:35,579 --> 00:16:36,660
in their units confront.

258
00:16:36,959 --> 00:16:43,920
One of our goals is to suggest at least what's the most appropriate or effective or at least

259
00:16:43,920 --> 00:16:53,079
some ideas for some good ways to respond or to react that will further the goal of leadership

260
00:16:53,079 --> 00:16:53,819
development.

261
00:16:53,820 --> 00:16:57,460
You know, scouting is a game with a purpose.

262
00:16:58,780 --> 00:17:06,440
And scout leadership, you know, for youth leadership, you have to turn it into a game for them so

263
00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:10,440
that they don't really realize that they're actually learning something, but it's a game.

264
00:17:11,680 --> 00:17:15,640
So the yarns are a good way to start.

265
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:17,500
Everybody likes a good story.

266
00:17:17,880 --> 00:17:21,840
There's a lot that I've read in the book that I've written on the blog in years past.

267
00:17:21,839 --> 00:17:25,419
We're all taking the lead from good old Baden-Powell.

268
00:17:26,159 --> 00:17:31,580
So it's not unusual that we should have some of the same ideas and things, but it's been

269
00:17:31,580 --> 00:17:32,360
pretty amazing.

270
00:17:33,639 --> 00:17:40,600
It's not, of course, that any of us are thinking that any of this is particularly unique.

271
00:17:40,600 --> 00:17:43,720
As you say, this is what Baden-Powell intended.

272
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:45,540
Yeah, he was there before any of us.

273
00:17:45,540 --> 00:17:51,760
He defined all this, and it seems like that at least some of us in the scouting organization

274
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:58,120
have gotten away from some of the basic things that were probably very clearly evident in

275
00:17:58,120 --> 00:17:59,260
1911.

276
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:02,360
It's just not as emphasized in the training materials.

277
00:18:02,580 --> 00:18:06,260
And it's kind of there, but it's not highlighted.

278
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:11,879
You know, I teach, this is Harry, I teach scoutmaster specifics.

279
00:18:11,880 --> 00:18:15,840
And the piece for the patrol method is, you know, you get 20 minutes.

280
00:18:16,060 --> 00:18:17,240
You just hit the highlights.

281
00:18:17,360 --> 00:18:21,940
You really can't, you can't teach the scoutmasters everything that they really need to know.

282
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:28,220
You talk about leadership training and mentoring as being something that is hands-on and not

283
00:18:28,220 --> 00:18:28,780
theoretical.

284
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:30,900
You take a young scout.

285
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:33,240
He's been in the classroom all day long.

286
00:18:33,300 --> 00:18:38,180
So when he comes to a scout meeting, you don't want to sitting down and talk leadership.

287
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:41,520
You want to put some of it into practical application.

288
00:18:42,040 --> 00:18:49,100
But you allow that 11, 12, 13-year-old boy that's in charge of his patrol, you know,

289
00:18:49,120 --> 00:18:50,060
kind of make a mistake.

290
00:18:50,140 --> 00:18:54,080
And then you're pulling off to the side and you say, okay, what could you have done a little

291
00:18:54,080 --> 00:18:55,400
bit better?

292
00:18:56,160 --> 00:18:57,220
What went right?

293
00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:58,600
What went wrong?

294
00:18:58,980 --> 00:19:02,880
And what can you do to improve on it the next time?

295
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:08,600
You let that, you let the boy kind of make those decisions, but you as the leader don't

296
00:19:08,600 --> 00:19:09,780
make them foreign.

297
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:12,380
Harry, you're telling me that these guys are going to make mistakes?

298
00:19:13,500 --> 00:19:17,080
You know, I don't know about too many leaders that don't make mistakes every day.

299
00:19:17,580 --> 00:19:22,820
We, as the leaders, have to be there to kind of pick them back up and say, yeah, okay, yeah,

300
00:19:22,820 --> 00:19:23,500
you made a mistake.

301
00:19:23,780 --> 00:19:24,100
All right?

302
00:19:24,180 --> 00:19:28,520
So, yeah, you got to be there to kind of coach them along and show them maybe a different

303
00:19:28,520 --> 00:19:28,960
direction.

304
00:19:28,960 --> 00:19:32,880
You spend a lot of time in the book talking history.

305
00:19:33,260 --> 00:19:37,000
Why is it important that we understand the history of scouting?

306
00:19:37,320 --> 00:19:40,260
Baden-Powell lived 100 years ago.

307
00:19:40,560 --> 00:19:43,520
And so you could look back and say, okay, well, yes, scouting is traditional.

308
00:19:43,760 --> 00:19:45,360
Of course, we've got all these great traditions.

309
00:19:45,900 --> 00:19:51,360
What did what Baden-Powell wrote almost a century ago, what does that really have to do with what's

310
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:52,700
going on in the 21st century?

311
00:19:52,700 --> 00:20:00,920
And the fact of the matter is that if you go back and read what Baden-Powell went through

312
00:20:00,920 --> 00:20:05,720
in order to create scouting, what influences did he have upon himself?

313
00:20:06,040 --> 00:20:07,260
Where did this all come from?

314
00:20:07,420 --> 00:20:12,760
What you end up finding is that he had a mixture of remarkable influences.

315
00:20:13,400 --> 00:20:18,560
And out of it, out of all of that came what he referred to as, you know, his experimental

316
00:20:18,560 --> 00:20:20,860
summer camp at Brown Sea Island.

317
00:20:20,860 --> 00:20:24,500
It wasn't just the first Boy Scout summer camp.

318
00:20:24,719 --> 00:20:30,520
It was actually the model for the successful implementation of the patrol method.

319
00:20:30,740 --> 00:20:32,580
That has never been done before.

320
00:20:32,740 --> 00:20:34,240
I mean, he was the first to do that.

321
00:20:34,479 --> 00:20:37,000
And it was revolutionary.

322
00:20:38,060 --> 00:20:42,360
You know, if you want to be an effective leader, you've got to know a little bit about scouting.

323
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:47,659
You know, so you've got to learn about Baden-Powell and some of the other great leaders that we've

324
00:20:47,659 --> 00:20:50,879
had, you know, within the scouting movement.

325
00:20:52,139 --> 00:20:55,540
You know, just like, you know, we teach U.S. history in our schools.

326
00:20:55,919 --> 00:20:56,659
Why is that?

327
00:20:56,800 --> 00:21:01,419
So that we can understand, you know, why our forefathers wrote the Constitution and the

328
00:21:01,419 --> 00:21:02,340
Declaration of Independence.

329
00:21:02,340 --> 00:21:07,000
There's something about the history of scouting that helps put things in context for us.

330
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:11,899
And then I was very impressed with a statement that you make in the book about stewardship

331
00:21:11,899 --> 00:21:14,879
and the stewardship of the scouting movement.

332
00:21:15,040 --> 00:21:16,320
What can you tell me about that?

333
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:23,360
Modern scout leaders are actually following in the footsteps of a century of highly illustrious,

334
00:21:23,919 --> 00:21:29,720
highly motivated, highly passionate, and experienced scout leaders that went before us.

335
00:21:29,720 --> 00:21:33,180
We are the ones who have now been handed the torch.

336
00:21:33,380 --> 00:21:34,120
We scout leaders.

337
00:21:34,700 --> 00:21:41,100
And we owe those in the program who came before us the responsibility to carry that torch as

338
00:21:41,100 --> 00:21:44,380
effectively as possible into this new century.

339
00:21:44,900 --> 00:21:46,960
Okay, so let me give you this scenario.

340
00:21:47,400 --> 00:21:54,160
I'm a brand new, newly minted scout leader, and you've got 30 seconds to talk to me.

341
00:21:54,840 --> 00:21:56,400
What are you going to tell me to do?

342
00:21:56,900 --> 00:21:58,320
Read the book.

343
00:21:59,720 --> 00:22:05,880
Go talk to your SPL and ask the SPL what he wants you to do.

344
00:22:06,680 --> 00:22:09,040
And hopefully the SPL will tell you to go sit in the corner.

345
00:22:09,140 --> 00:22:10,460
If we need your help, we'll let you know.

346
00:22:10,980 --> 00:22:17,780
What we say on page 10 of the book, there's a paragraph in there which actually arguably

347
00:22:17,780 --> 00:22:18,620
tells it all.

348
00:22:19,280 --> 00:22:25,480
It says, leadership training and mentoring in a scout troop is actually very simple.

349
00:22:25,480 --> 00:22:29,300
Any scout master who puts his mind to it can do it.

350
00:22:30,080 --> 00:22:32,120
Trust and respect your scouts.

351
00:22:32,800 --> 00:22:34,120
Use the patrol method.

352
00:22:34,800 --> 00:22:36,580
Give your scouts real responsibility.

353
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:38,120
Mentor.

354
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:39,360
Motivate.

355
00:22:40,080 --> 00:22:41,100
Recognize results.

356
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:44,980
What we would tell a new scout leader is do those things.

357
00:22:45,360 --> 00:22:46,960
Those are the important things to do.

358
00:22:46,960 --> 00:22:53,160
Look at the program from the standpoint of the boys and do those things.

359
00:22:53,160 --> 00:23:03,420
But in order to do those things right requires either experience, which obviously takes some time to get,

360
00:23:03,840 --> 00:23:12,180
or you can also go and talk to your scout master or district training people or other resources.

361
00:23:12,180 --> 00:23:17,200
And we also quote another thing in this book that Will Rogers once said.

362
00:23:17,280 --> 00:23:18,600
He said, there are three kinds of men.

363
00:23:18,680 --> 00:23:25,540
The ones that learn by reading, the few who learn by observation, and the rest of us have to pee on the electric fence and find out for ourselves.

364
00:23:26,040 --> 00:23:28,680
And that's what happens to most scout masters.

365
00:23:29,940 --> 00:23:35,780
It's a trial and error occupation, I guess you could say.

366
00:23:35,780 --> 00:23:42,520
So what we'd like to do is to give some new leaders a shortcut rather than having to learn everything the hard way.

367
00:23:42,620 --> 00:23:47,360
Maybe we can give them sort of a leg up on the learning curve as well as an orientation.

368
00:23:47,700 --> 00:23:54,680
So instead of heading off in a direction other than the one that will lead them to a scout-led and scout-run troop,

369
00:23:54,680 --> 00:24:02,100
we can sort of orient them in the right direction, that in their journey, in that direction toward a scout-led troop,

370
00:24:02,220 --> 00:24:12,300
that they will find a more rewarding and more effective leadership role for themselves.

371
00:24:12,760 --> 00:24:17,540
In particular, they'll be much more effective in teaching their scouts how to become leaders.

372
00:24:17,540 --> 00:24:24,780
So at one point, you guys were both newly minted adult leaders, and you jumped in with both feet.

373
00:24:25,360 --> 00:24:32,120
What has changed about your approach from that first day to what you're doing now?

374
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:39,000
I think we can both say that radical changes have occurred since each of us began being scout leaders.

375
00:24:39,080 --> 00:24:39,340
Yes.

376
00:24:39,340 --> 00:24:49,600
We are both very energetic, sort of potentially do-it-all kind of people who have ideas.

377
00:24:50,120 --> 00:24:53,240
And I think that when we both started as newly minted adult leaders,

378
00:24:53,459 --> 00:25:02,240
we were not effective in terms of developing a scout-led and scout-run program.

379
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:13,860
We were acting as patrol leaders ourselves, as adult patrol leaders, as opposed to scout leaders who will allow the boys to be the patrol leaders and the boys to be the leaders.

380
00:25:13,860 --> 00:25:16,760
Well, isn't it kind of messy when you let the boys do that?

381
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:19,000
That's the fun part.

382
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:23,040
That's the fun part, you know.

383
00:25:23,040 --> 00:25:28,399
Well, you know, but that's, you know, I'm a retired command sergeant major after 30 years in the Army.

384
00:25:29,040 --> 00:25:36,340
A leader, and they, you know, they say the big difference between the Army and Boy Scouts is that the Boy Scouts have adult leaders.

385
00:25:38,260 --> 00:25:43,580
Every time that one of my non-commissioned officers made a mistake, if I crucified him, they wouldn't learn.

386
00:25:43,700 --> 00:25:45,300
They would shut down.

387
00:25:45,680 --> 00:25:48,540
And I find the same thing with the boys.

388
00:25:48,540 --> 00:25:55,180
The boys, if you always are telling them that this is what you did wrong, this is what you did wrong, this is what you did wrong, this is what you did wrong,

389
00:25:55,520 --> 00:26:03,300
well, then they're going to stop doing anything because all they know is, oh, I'm just going to get hollered at by Mr. Wimbrough

390
00:26:03,300 --> 00:26:06,080
because it's not being done his way.

391
00:26:06,080 --> 00:26:17,980
And that's where I had to step back and really take a look and kind of adjust my leadership style from the Army to Boy Scouts, to scouting.

392
00:26:17,980 --> 00:26:21,700
And it works in both places.

393
00:26:23,160 --> 00:26:23,640
Yes.

394
00:26:24,520 --> 00:26:28,400
It works in scouting, and it worked in the Army, too.

395
00:26:28,480 --> 00:26:35,500
When I first became a non-commissioned officer at the age of 19, I was a pretty hard charge in NCO.

396
00:26:35,500 --> 00:26:45,200
So I had a wise squad leader and platoon sergeant pull me off to the side and say, you know, Sergeant Wimbrough, he says, you're going to go far in this man's army.

397
00:26:46,040 --> 00:26:51,620
However, you need to change some of your leadership style.

398
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:53,600
So I did.

399
00:26:53,600 --> 00:27:04,219
There was a particularly interesting section of the book, and section seven is called Planning, How to Facilitate Without Taking Over.

400
00:27:04,379 --> 00:27:09,320
How do you facilitate youth planning without just kind of jumping in and doing it for them?

401
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:15,379
Our experience, Clark, is that that actually is one of the most difficult things for a scoutmaster to do.

402
00:27:15,679 --> 00:27:24,220
Scouts that are age, you know, 14, 15, patrol leader age are not generally very good at long-range planning.

403
00:27:24,439 --> 00:27:27,260
Their time window for planning is totally different from adults.

404
00:27:27,260 --> 00:27:34,860
You know, oftentimes scouts are worrying about what they're going to have for lunch, you know, five minutes before they're supposed to eat it.

405
00:27:35,340 --> 00:27:38,900
You know, we're asking them to do some planning where, so it's October.

406
00:27:39,040 --> 00:27:44,800
Next June, we're going to go to, you know, we're going to go caving next June, and we better start making the arrangements.

407
00:27:44,940 --> 00:27:46,820
And they'll look at you like you have to have your head examined.

408
00:27:47,080 --> 00:27:47,480
What do you mean?

409
00:27:48,120 --> 00:27:49,140
That's next June.

410
00:27:49,280 --> 00:27:50,379
What are you talking about?

411
00:27:50,460 --> 00:27:51,500
Why are we worried about that?

412
00:27:51,680 --> 00:27:54,300
That's a lifetime away.

413
00:27:54,300 --> 00:28:00,740
If you're a program director for a camporee, you're planning that camporee a year out.

414
00:28:00,980 --> 00:28:02,840
And you know about it at troop meetings.

415
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:08,180
And the boys, just an example, we had a camporee last weekend.

416
00:28:08,899 --> 00:28:11,500
The boys started really planning for it two weeks ago.

417
00:28:12,520 --> 00:28:22,320
And then the meeting before the camporee is they finalized their meal plans and figured out who was going to buy what food and what time they were going to meet.

418
00:28:22,320 --> 00:28:24,020
They had it well under control.

419
00:28:24,300 --> 00:28:28,780
As adults, we do long-term planning and short-term planning.

420
00:28:29,120 --> 00:28:31,040
The boys plan, when am I going to eat?

421
00:28:31,260 --> 00:28:32,180
When am I going to sleep?

422
00:28:32,899 --> 00:28:33,800
When am I going to eat?

423
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:34,899
When am I going to sleep?

424
00:28:35,740 --> 00:28:37,180
I have a pair of socks.

425
00:28:37,300 --> 00:28:37,680
It's good.

426
00:28:38,020 --> 00:28:39,800
I don't have to worry about anything else.

427
00:28:40,560 --> 00:28:43,260
Have you been talking to my scouts, Harry?

428
00:28:43,399 --> 00:28:44,780
And I've been talking to my son.

429
00:28:44,780 --> 00:28:59,300
But one thing that the boys, we're going to go camping, but they don't think about the tour permits, safety forms, and all the health forms and stuff like that.

430
00:28:59,300 --> 00:29:08,680
And that's where you've got to kind of coach them along on that area until when they start getting used to doing some of the planning.

431
00:29:08,680 --> 00:29:15,379
Then they can say, oh, we need to talk to somebody on the committee to get our tour permit.

432
00:29:15,380 --> 00:29:18,400
We need to make sure that we have the permission forms.

433
00:29:18,860 --> 00:29:20,600
But it takes time.

434
00:29:20,760 --> 00:29:25,380
And it takes the coaching and the mentoring of an adult leader to be able to do that.

435
00:29:25,380 --> 00:29:34,160
And it's actually a very valuable life skill that an adult leader will be teaching his scouts in taking them through that exercise.

436
00:29:34,500 --> 00:29:37,080
It seems to us that there's some pretty important things involved.

437
00:29:37,220 --> 00:29:41,200
One of them is the expectation of how much planning.

438
00:29:41,200 --> 00:29:53,080
So adults have an expectation of probably much more intense and much more detailed planning than any scout is going to have, unless maybe they're an older scout.

439
00:29:53,080 --> 00:30:04,540
And also one of the points we make in our book is that, well, depending upon what it is, you may not need that intense level of planning.

440
00:30:04,540 --> 00:30:18,280
So, for example, if you're a type A scout master and you don't want to disappoint the parents by making your unit look like it's disorganized, you want everything organized and running like clockwork, that means you need a very detailed plan.

441
00:30:18,379 --> 00:30:21,560
So that way everyone knows everything that's going to happen in advance.

442
00:30:21,560 --> 00:30:25,480
Then our suggestion is, well, okay, look at what the objective is.

443
00:30:25,720 --> 00:30:31,340
So if it's just a weekend camp out at a local park or whatever, do you need that level of planning?

444
00:30:31,879 --> 00:30:39,940
Or would you rather leave it up a little bit up in the air and let the scouts work through it and then actually learn from the experience?

445
00:30:40,659 --> 00:30:42,580
Sometimes you do need that level of planning.

446
00:30:42,639 --> 00:30:51,100
If you're going to be doing something pretty intense, you know, some kind of high adventure program where you are worried about health and safety kind of issues, absolutely.

447
00:30:51,100 --> 00:30:55,820
An adult needs to make sure that those details are locked down and solid.

448
00:30:55,820 --> 00:31:13,860
But it does seem to be helpful for a scout master to talk with the parents and to create an expectation that, look, this is not an adult-run and adult-planned unit here.

449
00:31:13,860 --> 00:31:22,479
We give our scouts major responsibility for planning and execution.

450
00:31:23,080 --> 00:31:28,340
And if things don't go 100% perfectly, well, guess what?

451
00:31:29,540 --> 00:31:30,919
That's the way it's supposed to be.

452
00:31:31,540 --> 00:31:35,419
And as long as the boys have a good time, they probably planned it that way.

453
00:31:35,420 --> 00:31:38,140
Well, I'm going to be the devil's advocate here for a moment.

454
00:31:38,580 --> 00:31:48,160
I mean, honestly, guys, I can expect a 15-, 16-, 17-year-old scout to be thinking about things like tour permits and permission slips and stuff like that.

455
00:31:48,240 --> 00:31:49,640
I mean, I'm the scout master.

456
00:31:50,160 --> 00:31:51,080
Isn't that my job?

457
00:31:51,160 --> 00:31:52,460
Isn't that what I'm supposed to be doing?

458
00:31:52,460 --> 00:31:55,980
So at that point, it ends up being an issue of responsibility.

459
00:31:55,980 --> 00:32:09,480
And we agree, Clark, that it's going to be probably an undue expectation for a 14-, 15-year-old scout to be worried about a tour permit

460
00:32:09,480 --> 00:32:17,620
and whether all the medical forms are filed and are brought along with the unit.

461
00:32:17,620 --> 00:32:21,159
But at the same time, they shouldn't be oblivious to it either.

462
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:29,219
Maybe he's not getting the tour permit, but he knows the adult on the committee that gets the tour permits for us.

463
00:32:29,600 --> 00:32:33,000
And that becomes a teaching point, that teaching and coaching.

464
00:32:33,580 --> 00:32:38,879
You know, you might want to say, hey, have you talked to the tour permit committee member?

465
00:32:39,320 --> 00:32:44,219
So it becomes more of a teaching point than really for him to be a player.

466
00:32:44,280 --> 00:32:47,139
But it's something for him to think about as he goes along.

467
00:32:47,140 --> 00:32:57,160
So maybe the next time, when he does, as a patrol leader, plans an outing, he knows that, you know, I need to talk to Mr. Smith about tour permits.

468
00:32:57,540 --> 00:32:58,620
I can see that, Harry.

469
00:32:58,740 --> 00:33:01,180
But like I said, I'm still being a devil's advocate here.

470
00:33:01,580 --> 00:33:02,740
That makes sense.

471
00:33:02,840 --> 00:33:06,980
But isn't that just, as a scout master, isn't that just easier for me to do myself?

472
00:33:06,980 --> 00:33:08,820
Yeah, it'd be easier for you to do it yourself.

473
00:33:09,040 --> 00:33:12,860
But what are you teaching the boy to do it yourself?

474
00:33:12,860 --> 00:33:19,060
And actually, Clark, that's actually a great comment, because you could extend that to just about everything we're talking about.

475
00:33:19,500 --> 00:33:21,760
Isn't it easier for the scout master to do it himself?

476
00:33:21,979 --> 00:33:25,120
Isn't it easier for the scout master to define the whole plan for the whole year?

477
00:33:25,540 --> 00:33:29,919
Isn't it easier for the scout master to tell patrol leaders exactly what they're supposed to be doing?

478
00:33:30,340 --> 00:33:36,080
Isn't it easier to just bypass the patrol leaders and tell the scouts precisely what they're supposed to be doing?

479
00:33:36,080 --> 00:33:38,240
That's not the point at all.

480
00:33:38,240 --> 00:33:40,379
It is easier.

481
00:33:40,540 --> 00:33:44,399
It is easier for a scout master to have an adult-run troop.

482
00:33:44,780 --> 00:33:47,480
There is no doubt in my mind that that's the case.

483
00:33:48,020 --> 00:33:51,060
It is easier for a scout master to have an adult-run troop.

484
00:33:51,480 --> 00:33:57,980
Much less trouble, much less coordination, much more straightforward to have an adult-run troop.

485
00:33:58,699 --> 00:34:00,780
A scout-run troop, that's hard.

486
00:34:00,780 --> 00:34:09,120
A scout-run troop is harder, because now the adult, now the scout leaders have to start thinking about their interventions.

487
00:34:09,900 --> 00:34:18,199
Do we really want to step in and command this or direct that or structure this?

488
00:34:18,300 --> 00:34:23,960
Or is this something that the scouts are able to do, perfectly capable of doing?

489
00:34:24,480 --> 00:34:29,580
And yet if we step in as adults, the scouts will just back right off and let the adult do it.

490
00:34:29,580 --> 00:34:34,639
I mean, I think most experienced scout masters would almost certainly say the same thing.

491
00:34:34,719 --> 00:34:35,100
Oh, yeah.

492
00:34:35,620 --> 00:34:36,500
Adult-run troops.

493
00:34:36,659 --> 00:34:37,319
That's easy.

494
00:34:37,920 --> 00:34:39,000
Much easier.

495
00:34:39,400 --> 00:34:43,440
It's certainly a lot less wear and tear on the scout master's nerves.

496
00:34:43,940 --> 00:34:45,460
Right, and it also takes less time.

497
00:34:45,679 --> 00:34:48,819
I mean, the fact of the matter is that it takes more time to have a scout-run troop.

498
00:34:48,960 --> 00:34:49,440
It's like, wait a second.

499
00:34:49,799 --> 00:34:53,380
And actually, Baden Powell specifically said that.

500
00:34:53,699 --> 00:34:58,680
One of the things that he wrote, I can't quote it exactly, but he said something up to paraphrase.

501
00:34:58,680 --> 00:35:04,339
He said that what you're supposed to do is to allow your patrol leaders to do as much as possible.

502
00:35:04,480 --> 00:35:08,680
It's not so much to save the scout master time.

503
00:35:09,100 --> 00:35:10,980
It's to train the scout.

504
00:35:11,560 --> 00:35:12,700
And that's what the point is.

505
00:35:12,960 --> 00:35:16,740
I continue in my advocacy role for the devil.

506
00:35:16,740 --> 00:35:24,779
But parents in our troop are just not going to put up with me sitting around and not doing these things.

507
00:35:24,779 --> 00:35:31,180
My committee is going to go crazy if I tell them that we're going to let the boys plan and lead these things.

508
00:35:31,379 --> 00:35:32,839
They're expecting me to do that.

509
00:35:32,939 --> 00:35:34,879
I mean, how do I handle a situation like that?

510
00:35:34,880 --> 00:35:39,060
As the scout master, you have to tell them, you know, go sit in the corner.

511
00:35:39,540 --> 00:35:40,680
Let the boys do it.

512
00:35:41,340 --> 00:35:45,740
Let the boys come to you and ask you for what they need from the committee.

513
00:35:45,820 --> 00:35:47,240
The committee is there to support.

514
00:35:47,620 --> 00:35:53,460
Parents do more for their children today than when you and I were growing up.

515
00:35:53,460 --> 00:36:07,680
And so they have to allow their boys to spread their wings a little bit earlier than what they normally would at the age of 18 or 19.

516
00:36:08,440 --> 00:36:12,520
And so you just got to pull them and say this is a boy.

517
00:36:12,920 --> 00:36:14,260
Let the boys do it.

518
00:36:14,860 --> 00:36:19,260
You know, yes, it may look like chaos, but it's a boy controlled chaos.

519
00:36:19,260 --> 00:36:30,400
The other thing that I would say is that that I agree, Clark, that is that your question is one of the biggest quandaries of a scout master who wants to have a scout led troop.

520
00:36:30,780 --> 00:36:31,280
Wait a minute.

521
00:36:31,400 --> 00:36:32,400
I'm the scout master.

522
00:36:32,960 --> 00:36:35,500
All the parents are expecting me to be the leader.

523
00:36:36,120 --> 00:36:38,620
All the scouts are expecting me to be the leader.

524
00:36:38,700 --> 00:36:40,920
The program is expecting me to be the leader.

525
00:36:41,500 --> 00:36:42,500
That means what?

526
00:36:42,500 --> 00:36:48,740
Does that mean that that I, as scout master, need to stand up in front, make all the all the important decisions?

527
00:36:48,740 --> 00:36:53,220
Or is there another approach where it's the boys who are up in front?

528
00:36:53,339 --> 00:36:56,799
It's the boys who are making the important decisions.

529
00:36:57,100 --> 00:36:59,700
It's the boys who are the most visible leaders.

530
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:03,799
And I'll just quote from something which Ronald Reagan said.

531
00:37:03,859 --> 00:37:05,319
We've got this quote in our book as well.

532
00:37:05,379 --> 00:37:13,680
He says, there's no limit to what a man can do or where he can go if he doesn't mind who gets the credit.

533
00:37:13,680 --> 00:37:21,700
The scouts are the ones who should be getting the credit for the successes and taking the responsibilities for things that don't work out all that well.

534
00:37:22,299 --> 00:37:33,680
So if I invest my scouts with the authority and the responsibility to plan these things, aren't they going to miss some stuff?

535
00:37:33,680 --> 00:37:39,960
I mean, wouldn't they get more out of it if I was planning outings and things?

536
00:37:40,600 --> 00:37:41,960
Aren't they going to miss opportunities?

537
00:37:42,200 --> 00:37:42,799
Just the opposite.

538
00:37:44,140 --> 00:37:45,799
Just the opposite.

539
00:37:45,800 --> 00:38:02,900
If I, as the adult leader, plan everything, being the type of person that I am, I've written a five-paragraph operations order and have all my execution checklists and execution matrix so that I know everything is going.

540
00:38:03,020 --> 00:38:05,880
I've got to have this, this, and everything is going well.

541
00:38:06,180 --> 00:38:06,400
Yeah.

542
00:38:06,720 --> 00:38:07,020
Okay.

543
00:38:07,280 --> 00:38:07,600
Great.

544
00:38:07,600 --> 00:38:24,900
But if the boys plan it and say they, you know, they're going to learn from their mistakes, they actually might miss some opportunities in one respect and gain immeasurably other opportunities on the other side.

545
00:38:24,900 --> 00:38:44,300
So in a world where our scouts, at least many of them in many parts of the countries, are expecting to be entertained by adults and programmed up the wazoo, out the wazoo by the adults to put the scouts in charge and have responsibility for figuring out what they're going to do.

546
00:38:44,920 --> 00:38:45,880
That's an opportunity.

547
00:38:45,880 --> 00:38:54,280
If I'm not making sure that it's, you know, really a whiz-bang program and everything, aren't they going to just leave the troop?

548
00:38:54,280 --> 00:39:02,420
How am I going to attract new scouts if the scouts are just caught up in leading and doing all this stuff all the time?

549
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:04,020
I mean, they won't be doing anything fun.

550
00:39:04,280 --> 00:39:04,380
That's the key.

551
00:39:05,080 --> 00:39:11,500
They are doing fun things because they're the ones that are planning the program with the resources that you've given them.

552
00:39:11,840 --> 00:39:19,680
With the boys planning the program, with the boys executing their program, that's what's going to keep them active.

553
00:39:19,680 --> 00:39:25,520
Because if you as the adult planet, they're saying, huh, okay, yep, here we go again.

554
00:39:25,620 --> 00:39:28,940
We're doing the same stuff and we're not doing what we want to do.

555
00:39:29,379 --> 00:39:35,480
And the other thing I would add is that it's not like the scoutmaster just sits back and lets the program fall apart.

556
00:39:35,680 --> 00:39:42,980
You might need to do that on occasion in order to make sure the boys understand that it's their responsibility, that if they don't do it, it's not happening.

557
00:39:42,980 --> 00:39:50,200
But this is where the mentoring and the coaching and the advising comes in, where you don't tell them what to do.

558
00:39:50,780 --> 00:39:56,820
You do what any respectful leader will do when training a new leader, okay?

559
00:39:57,540 --> 00:39:59,040
Let me show you some resources.

560
00:39:59,620 --> 00:40:01,040
Let your scout develop it.

561
00:40:01,320 --> 00:40:03,820
And at that point, you're not the one who's dictating it.

562
00:40:03,940 --> 00:40:04,880
He's deciding it.

563
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:06,520
He's got responsibility for it.

564
00:40:06,620 --> 00:40:07,520
He'll execute it.

565
00:40:07,680 --> 00:40:08,620
It'll be great.

566
00:40:09,140 --> 00:40:10,500
He'll take the credit for it.

567
00:40:10,500 --> 00:40:12,100
He'll have a big smile on his face.

568
00:40:12,160 --> 00:40:17,140
He'll have learned a ton of stuff about himself and about how to be an effective leader.

569
00:40:17,140 --> 00:40:28,640
So you guys must have a troop of just who are some kind of superhero scouts because my scouts, they wouldn't be able to do this, would they?

570
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:34,060
You know, any scouts would be able to do it.

571
00:40:34,360 --> 00:40:38,040
And no, our own troop is not superheroes or anything like that.

572
00:40:38,040 --> 00:40:50,560
So obviously, it doesn't always work 100% because basically these scouts who are planning these program events are inexperienced and they have a lot to learn.

573
00:40:51,279 --> 00:41:05,840
And so in the course of learning, they may not be able to put on something that is as perfect and clockwork as an experienced type A adult who is planning the whole thing from beginning to end and thought of every detail.

574
00:41:06,220 --> 00:41:07,620
It's just not going to be like that.

575
00:41:07,620 --> 00:41:18,220
It's more going to be like making sausage as opposed to having some pristine, elegant, finished product that just hand it off to the scouts and say, okay, you guys execute this.

576
00:41:18,339 --> 00:41:20,980
If you do this, it'll be great.

577
00:41:20,980 --> 00:41:25,560
But you are sacrificing something.

578
00:41:25,660 --> 00:41:26,620
There's no doubt about it.

579
00:41:26,620 --> 00:41:44,460
In a scout run troop, at least until you end up being able to retain the 16 and 17-year-olds who are now in the habit of taking responsibility and making these kind of decisions.

580
00:41:44,460 --> 00:41:45,520
There's going to be a lag.

581
00:41:46,880 --> 00:41:49,380
And this is where persistence comes in.

582
00:41:49,380 --> 00:41:54,240
This is where you have to keep your eye on the objective as a scout master.

583
00:41:54,320 --> 00:41:55,500
What are you trying to accomplish here?

584
00:41:55,940 --> 00:41:58,500
Are you trying to have a 100% perfect program?

585
00:41:59,080 --> 00:42:03,520
Or are you trying to train new leaders?

586
00:42:03,520 --> 00:42:09,320
So I've been to the training and I watched the videos and everything looks really perfect in those videos.

587
00:42:09,320 --> 00:42:13,620
And I showed this to my scouts and I went and I worked with them on this.

588
00:42:13,800 --> 00:42:16,020
And they're looking at it like a dog with a new pan.

589
00:42:16,080 --> 00:42:18,400
They don't seem to understand any of it.

590
00:42:18,620 --> 00:42:19,940
They don't plan anything.

591
00:42:20,240 --> 00:42:21,480
They don't do anything.

592
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:23,580
So what am I supposed to do?

593
00:42:23,940 --> 00:42:24,300
Yeah.

594
00:42:24,400 --> 00:42:24,740
I mean.

595
00:42:24,920 --> 00:42:25,920
That's a tough one, isn't it?

596
00:42:25,920 --> 00:42:26,600
Yeah.

597
00:42:27,500 --> 00:42:28,220
Well, yeah.

598
00:42:28,360 --> 00:42:36,880
But maybe what you have to do is take a boy that's not a patrol leader yet but is maybe a first-class scout.

599
00:42:37,960 --> 00:42:39,960
He wants to be a patrol leader.

600
00:42:40,320 --> 00:42:46,180
So you're sending off to the NYLT, the National Youth Leadership Force.

601
00:42:46,640 --> 00:42:50,740
That will kind of get him excited about the program.

602
00:42:50,740 --> 00:43:02,100
And he brings it back in kind of making like a leadership trainer so that he can get an advisor to other patrol leaders.

603
00:43:02,180 --> 00:43:04,379
Kind of like a troop guide for leadership.

604
00:43:05,180 --> 00:43:14,439
If the boys just want to sit around and let the adults do it, well, then what you do is you plan a weekend where you just go and you sit around.

605
00:43:14,440 --> 00:43:18,840
And when the boys say, well, this wasn't very much fun, then you say, okay.

606
00:43:20,180 --> 00:43:27,380
If you had to plan something, we might have been able to have some fun activities while we were on this camping trip.

607
00:43:27,740 --> 00:43:29,780
But you left it all up to me as the adult.

608
00:43:29,900 --> 00:43:36,700
The only thing I wanted to do was go out to the woods and sit on the rock next to the fire.

609
00:43:37,780 --> 00:43:39,240
For me, that's a fun time.

610
00:43:39,240 --> 00:43:47,279
But, you know, maybe before that, you have a conversation with your patrol leader's council.

611
00:43:47,919 --> 00:43:51,060
Say, gentlemen, this is your weekend.

612
00:43:52,120 --> 00:43:53,279
You're going to plan this weekend.

613
00:43:54,540 --> 00:44:04,799
And whatever you plan within reason, as long as there's no issues with health and safety, I, as your scoutmaster, are going to go along with it.

614
00:44:05,180 --> 00:44:07,160
But it's your responsibility to plan.

615
00:44:07,160 --> 00:44:17,899
And so you challenge them and make them understand that maybe for the first time in their whole lives, they're able to actually plan their own time for a whole weekend.

616
00:44:18,440 --> 00:44:20,000
They get to do what they want to do.

617
00:44:20,180 --> 00:44:22,740
It's their responsibility and it's their privilege to do it.

618
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:26,000
And no adult is going to tell them, oh, you can't do that.

619
00:44:26,500 --> 00:44:27,500
You can't do this.

620
00:44:28,020 --> 00:44:30,399
You have to do this and you have to do that.

621
00:44:30,400 --> 00:44:38,520
And that is an amazing opportunity for a 21st century, you know, 15-year-old.

622
00:44:38,660 --> 00:44:43,640
Because most of them, many of them, at least in many parts of the country, many of them never get that opportunity.

623
00:44:43,640 --> 00:44:54,140
If I do this, if I tell them that they can do anything they want to do, all they're going to do is sit around and play video games and, you know, they'll just eat whatever they want to eat.

624
00:44:54,240 --> 00:44:56,299
And they probably won't get any requirements done.

625
00:44:57,160 --> 00:45:03,279
And, you know, so how in the world can I just let them have carte blanche to go and do whatever they want to do?

626
00:45:03,280 --> 00:45:08,500
If you just go and do requirements for the weekend, well, that's not the right way.

627
00:45:08,880 --> 00:45:14,540
You go and you do an event and then you have them look at their book and see what requirements that they might have did.

628
00:45:15,320 --> 00:45:20,720
And the other thing is that if you challenge your scouts to come up with something, you'll be amazed.

629
00:45:21,460 --> 00:45:21,820
Exactly.

630
00:45:21,940 --> 00:45:22,520
You'll be amazed.

631
00:45:22,920 --> 00:45:30,380
Sometimes, often actually, they will come up with something that you as a scoutmaster would never have thought of in a million years.

632
00:45:30,380 --> 00:45:33,180
It's not because it's not appropriate.

633
00:45:33,400 --> 00:45:34,500
It's not because it's not safe.

634
00:45:34,980 --> 00:45:37,880
It's entirely safe and it's entirely appropriate.

635
00:45:38,840 --> 00:45:39,640
And it's theirs.

636
00:45:39,940 --> 00:45:40,840
It's not your idea.

637
00:45:40,940 --> 00:45:41,740
It's their idea.

638
00:45:42,500 --> 00:45:55,020
And it might not be something that you as a, you know, as a 40 or 50 or whatever year old scoutmaster might think is what those boys are going to want to do.

639
00:45:55,320 --> 00:45:59,860
So if you had been dictating it, you would never in a million years have thought of doing that.

640
00:45:59,860 --> 00:46:02,040
But it ends up being a blast for them.

641
00:46:02,180 --> 00:46:03,600
They absolutely love it.

642
00:46:03,680 --> 00:46:04,400
And they own it.

643
00:46:04,640 --> 00:46:06,080
It's theirs and they own it.

644
00:46:06,680 --> 00:46:10,820
We're not wired the same way as a 14, 15-year-old is.

645
00:46:11,580 --> 00:46:14,160
They're wired just a little bit different.

646
00:46:14,760 --> 00:46:22,080
And sometimes it's hard to remember that we were that 13, 14-year-old boy doing the same thing.

647
00:46:22,480 --> 00:46:26,740
Talk to me a little bit about, you have a section about caring leadership.

648
00:46:27,920 --> 00:46:29,440
What is caring leadership?

649
00:46:29,440 --> 00:46:30,820
Tell leaders three things.

650
00:46:31,580 --> 00:46:33,040
One, lead from the front.

651
00:46:33,660 --> 00:46:36,700
Set the example and take care of your people.

652
00:46:37,080 --> 00:46:38,380
You've got to know them.

653
00:46:38,440 --> 00:46:41,720
Everybody that's in your patrol is a valuable member.

654
00:46:42,400 --> 00:46:48,100
But you as a leader, you have to know what's his likes and dislikes.

655
00:46:48,100 --> 00:47:00,960
And caring leadership actually is one of the most important things to get across to our young leaders because it really is part of the key to the whole thing.

656
00:47:00,960 --> 00:47:12,880
Anyone who's studied and worked with adolescents, anyone who's tried to have anything to do with the scout troop knows that a 14- or 15-year-old boy is pretty self-absorbed.

657
00:47:13,340 --> 00:47:17,620
They're thinking, oftentimes, they're thinking about one person and that's themselves.

658
00:47:17,620 --> 00:47:26,299
To get them to think about the 11-year-old who's just joined the troop and needs some mentoring and some help.

659
00:47:26,300 --> 00:47:28,920
And that's important.

660
00:47:29,340 --> 00:47:42,860
And that's, once again, that's something that at least some scouts, it's going to be a real revelation for them that they're supposed to be thinking about younger scouts.

661
00:47:42,940 --> 00:47:45,820
They're supposed to be caring about those younger kids.

662
00:47:45,820 --> 00:47:47,640
But that's what our program is based on.

663
00:47:48,080 --> 00:47:53,240
I think, fellas, that we've just scratched the surface of what's in the book.

664
00:47:53,700 --> 00:47:56,820
And it's arranged in a way that's very accessible.

665
00:47:57,480 --> 00:48:00,920
I think it's just an excellent resource for scout leaders.

666
00:48:01,280 --> 00:48:02,660
So let me ask you this, guys.

667
00:48:03,400 --> 00:48:14,220
If you get discouraged and you get down and things aren't going quite so well as a scout leader, what makes you keep at it?

668
00:48:14,220 --> 00:48:18,540
What do you think about that kind of reanimates you and gets you moving again?

669
00:48:19,040 --> 00:48:25,240
Although we can take inspiration from people like Baden-Powell, and that certainly is one place to go.

670
00:48:25,720 --> 00:48:29,520
But the other place to go is just to watch your boys.

671
00:48:29,819 --> 00:48:36,060
Watch how they're interacting with one another and reflect on the huge potential there.

672
00:48:36,299 --> 00:48:41,359
And that you, as a scout leader, can have a major impact on their lives.

673
00:48:41,360 --> 00:48:47,460
And so when you start thinking about that, it's pretty hard to stay discouraged for long.

674
00:48:47,980 --> 00:48:59,020
When I get discouraged, one of the things that keeps me going is when you sit in a scout master conference and you talk to a boy.

675
00:48:59,880 --> 00:49:02,980
And you're asking what he wants to do.

676
00:49:03,540 --> 00:49:05,840
And he says, I want to be an Eagle Scout.

677
00:49:05,840 --> 00:49:10,420
And you're asking, well, why do you want to be an Eagle Scout?

678
00:49:10,640 --> 00:49:14,420
He says, because scouting and Eagle Scouts make a difference.

679
00:49:15,220 --> 00:49:19,360
And that kind of rejuvenates me a little bit.

680
00:49:19,500 --> 00:49:29,760
And just looking at the boys and their smiles, you know, at the end of a camping trip when it's been raining and you've been miserable,

681
00:49:29,760 --> 00:49:32,520
and they say, oh, man, was that a great time or what?

682
00:49:34,560 --> 00:49:38,500
The best times always have rain involved, apparently.

683
00:49:38,600 --> 00:49:38,860
Always.

684
00:49:38,960 --> 00:49:40,200
I mean, that's my experience.

685
00:49:40,880 --> 00:49:41,440
Or snow.

686
00:49:41,640 --> 00:49:42,160
Or snow.

687
00:49:42,700 --> 00:49:43,000
Yeah.

688
00:49:44,760 --> 00:49:45,960
Rob, I'll start with you.

689
00:49:46,020 --> 00:49:47,280
